---
epr-meta-version: 1
id: eprfs-storage-byte-transport
purpose: >
  Adapter for existing content-addressed byte and evidence transport. Storage
  availability and retention are distinct from the authority that elects a release.
rules:
  - id: storage-possession-is-not-election
    class: inject
    when:
      write: "*.rs"
      contains-any: ["fetch_blob", "put_blob", "publish_attestation", "StorageEndpoint"]
    dedupe-of: "elohim/eprfs/eprfs-core/src/storage.rs (EprfsStorage byte interface)"
    why: >
      Extend eprfs-storage through existing storage transport for immutable bytes and
      evidence. Verify a received CID before persisting or serving its bytes. A peer
      possessing a blob does not elect its release head or confer standing; content
      governance owns lineage, delegation, revocation and observed election. Reuse
      the existing transport and retention path rather than adding a new authority
      store or protocol. MemoryStorage is test scaffolding; DirectoryStorage is
      an explicit private local CAS, not a completed peer-delivery implementation.
---

# eprfs-storage — bytes, not release authority

`MemoryStorage` is a test/prototyping adapter. `DirectoryStorage` verifies immutable
local CID-named files and refuses corrupt or public Unix stores/objects; it does
not resolve EPRs or publish attestations. The caller owns and trusts its directory
against hostile path replacement. Successful puts sync the verified object inode,
store directory and store parent, including already-present objects and a competing
writer's winner. Creating a root syncs it and its existing parent even if another
creator won; opening a root remains read-only and makes no durability claim.
Sync errors are errors, never successful custody. Incomplete temporary objects are
retained and named on write/sync failure; existing objects are never overwritten.
The 64 MiB object ceiling can be lowered but not raised. Version 0.3.3 preserves
legacy `put_blob` codec behavior and explicit verified raw/DAG-CBOR writes.
Persistence relies on the filesystem/device honoring `sync_all`; process-exit and
injected-error tests do not simulate physical power failure.
The approved peer-delivery
station will use existing transport to publish and retrieve verified bytes through a
second peer; that outcome needs a separate household receipt. Blob possession alone
does not establish an authorized release.
